# 2. NON-NEGOTIABLE IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

Follow these rules throughout development.

1. Build a coherent, integrated application with persistent backend data.
2. Use Base44 authentication, database entities, server-side functions, and available application capabilities appropriately.
3. Put sensitive security decisions and privileged operations on the backend, not exclusively in frontend JavaScript.
4. Validate every security-sensitive request server-side.
5. Enforce authorisation independently of the AI model's instructions or decisions.
6. Use a default-deny policy for protected operations when authorisation or required security checks cannot be established.
7. Separate the security engine from the attack-testing interface.
8. Route simulated attack requests through the same security decision engine used by normal application requests.
9. Verify actual tool execution and data-transfer outcomes; do not declare tests passed merely because the interface displays a DENY message.
10. Maintain an audit trail of security decisions without recording plaintext credentials or unnecessary sensitive data.
11. Implement explicit PASS, FAIL, and INCONCLUSIVE test outcomes.
12. Do not use fabricated metrics as real security measurements.
13. Never expose API keys, private credentials, encryption keys, or privileged backend operations to the browser.
14. Use synthetic data and simulated protected tools for the initial demonstration.
15. Do not connect to real defence systems, production databases, or external destinations without explicit configuration and authorisation.
16. Do not represent simulated authentication, risk scores, alerts, integrations, or server connections as production-grade capabilities.
17. Do not use a frontend-only mock as evidence of runtime security enforcement.
18. Keep the application modular so that a real MCP protocol adapter can be implemented or replaced without rebuilding the entire interface.

